{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Correction: the pack is NOT growing, the bridge budget is already blown, and authorship verification has been offline for days\n\nThree measurements taken from the failing CI runs themselves, which change this ticket's\nframing and supersede the \"headroom runway\" analysis posted earlier.\n\n### 1. The pack is flat, not growing\n\n`checkout pack is N KiB` read from five separate failing runs:\n\n| Run created | Pack (KiB) |\n|---|---|\n| 2026-09-15T12:57Z | 650,044 |\n| 2026-09-16T07:01Z | 647,945 |\n| 2026-09-17T01:13Z | 647,738 |\n| 2026-09-17T18:56Z | 648,166 |\n| 2026-09-18T07:00Z | 647,498 |\n\nAcross three days of heavy ticket writing the pack moved by **−2,546 KiB** — down, not up.\nThere is no growth curve and therefore no deadline. Every remedy I measured earlier was\nscored against reclaiming *future growth headroom*; that was the wrong question. The\nproblem is a **static overage**, and it has been static for at least three days.\n\n### 2. The bridge budget is already exceeded\n\n`verify-identity.yml:34` sets `REBAR_CHECKOUT_PACK_LIMIT_KIB: 563200`, and its own comment\nexplains the number: \"temporarily 563200 for the ~481 MiB blobless tickets pack … Restore\n102400 after the pack measures below that limit.\"\n\nThe pack now measures **~632 MiB against a bridge sized for ~481 MiB**. The W3 bridge\n(`a8ba`, closed) is not merely insufficient to reach the 100 MiB target — it no longer\nholds the current pack at all.\n\n### 3. The gate has not verified anything since at least 2026-09-15\n\nThis is the part that matters most, and it is not \"noise\". The pack check is the step\n**immediately after checkout**, before `setup-python`, before `Install rebar`, and before\nthe authorship verification itself. It fails closed. So for 12 consecutive runs the\nauthenticated-authorship gate has exited before verifying a single event.\n\nThe lane is not producing a weak signal — it is producing **no signal**, while looking like\na failing test. Whatever assurance this gate is supposed to provide about mutating events\nagainst the ancestry-scoped keyring, the project has not had it since 2026-09-15.\n\n### 4. My earlier remedy exploration duplicated `536b`'s already-decided design\n\n`536b` (`polite-antivirus-bedbug`) already owns this, with an operator decision recorded\n2026-08-24 (Option 2, remote-anchored horizon, N=30d) and a C1–C5 safety boundary. Its\ndescription also already states the two results I re-derived independently:\n\n- \"`git gc`/repack does NOT shrink it (proven)\" — matches the flat curve above.\n- \"`.retired` files are blobs; the guarded pack is blobless ⇒ `.retired` GC shrinks it ~0\"\n  — this is exactly why my archival measurement returned **0 KiB**. I reported that number\n  as a discovery; it was a re-derivation of a known result.\n\n`536b` further quantifies the achievable win: N=30d ≈ 365–380 MiB, N=7d ≈ 200–215 MiB, and\n\"≤100 MiB needs an unsafe <4-day horizon\" — so a temporary budget raise is unavoidable, and\nthe structural fixes are W1 (batch the enrichment/digest write lane) and W2 (shard the flat\nticket dirs to cut root-tree churn), both already carved out of that epic.\n\nThe decision I escalated from this ticket was therefore malformed: it offered a choice among\nremedies when the sanctioned remedy already exists, is designed, and is in progress.\n\n### Consequence for this ticket\n\nThe useful scope left here is not \"pick a reclamation strategy\". It is the gap between now\nand `536b` landing: the authorship gate is dark, and the bridge budget no longer matches\nreality. That is a scoping decision for the operator, not something to resolve by measuring\nmore remedies.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789715253570023500,"uuid":"db0a77ec-044b-474c-8e01-c5f7169d5fb3"}